[Chapter "Advance Variation"]
[Line "Van der Wiel Attack (4.Nc3)"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation, Van der Wiel Attack"]

{The sharpest try in the Advance: White throws the g-pawn forward. Black answers in the centre.} 1. e4 c6 {Prepares ...d5, so Black can challenge the centre with a pawn while the c8 bishop keeps its diagonal open.} 2. d4 d5 {The point of the Caro-Kann: hit e4 straight away, backed up by the c6 pawn.} 3. e5 Bf5 {Bishop out first, as always.} 4. Nc3 e6 {Calm development: it supports d5 and prepares ...c5. If g4 comes, the bishop drops back to g6.} 5. g4 {White gains space and chases the bishop.} 5... Bg6 {Retreat, but stay on the active diagonal.} 6. Nge2 c5 {When White commits to a kingside pawn storm, hit back in the centre at once.} *
